-- Q1: easy / L1 — Eligible trust types as S corporation shareholders
(3, 'S Corporations', $EXPL$Which of the following trust types is eligible to hold stock in an S corporation?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["A qualified Subchapter S trust (QSST)", "A foreign trust established under Canadian law", "A trust owned by a domestic C corporation", "A complex trust with no individual beneficiaries"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Section 1361(d)(1) specifically allows qualified Subchapter S trusts (QSSTs) to be S corporation shareholders, provided the trust meets requirements including having only one current income beneficiary who is a U.S. citizen or resident. Wrong (B): Foreign trusts are not eligible S corporation shareholders under Section 1361(b)(1)(B), which limits trust shareholders to certain domestic trusts. Wrong (C): A trust owned by a C corporation is not an eligible shareholder because the beneficial owner is a corporation, not an individual. Wrong (D): A complex trust with no individual beneficiaries does not qualify as an eligible S corporation shareholder under the permitted trust categories.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q2: hard / L4 — Deductible loss limited to stock basis plus direct loan (guarantee excluded)
(3, 'S Corporations', $EXPL$Ridgeway Corp, an S corporation, allocates a $120,000 ordinary loss to Colton, its only shareholder, for 2026. Colton's interest includes $45,000 of stock basis and a $25,000 note receivable from a direct loan he made to Ridgeway. Additionally, Colton personally guaranteed Ridgeway's $100,000 bank line of credit. Evaluate the maximum loss deductible under the Section 1366(d) basis limitation.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$45,000", "$70,000", "$170,000", "$120,000"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under Section 1366(d)(1), an S corporation shareholder may deduct losses only to the extent of stock basis plus debt basis from direct loans to the corporation. Colton's stock basis ($45,000) plus debt basis ($25,000) equals $70,000. The personal guarantee of the bank loan does not create debt basis for S corporation purposes, unlike partnership law. The remaining $50,000 is suspended. Wrong (A): This uses only stock basis and ignores the $25,000 direct loan, which also provides basis for loss deductions. Wrong (C): This incorrectly includes the $100,000 bank guarantee as part of basis; shareholder guarantees of third-party loans do not create S corporation debt basis. Wrong (D): The full $120,000 loss exceeds Colton's combined stock and debt basis of $70,000 and cannot be fully deducted.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q3: medium / L2 — Revocation filed within first 2.5 months effective current year
(3, 'S Corporations', $EXPL$On February 20, 2026, holders of 55% of Caldwell Corp's shares submit a revocation statement to the IRS to terminate the company's S election. Caldwell uses a calendar year. The statement does not specify a prospective date. When is the revocation effective?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["March 10, 2026", "March 15, 2026", "January 1, 2026", "January 1, 2027"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under Section 1362(d)(1)(C)(i), if a revocation is made on or before the 15th day of the third month of the tax year and no prospective date is specified, it is effective on the first day of the tax year. Since March 10 falls before March 15, the revocation is retroactively effective January 1, 2026. Wrong (A): The revocation is not effective on the date of filing; it relates back to the first day of the tax year when filed within the first 2.5 months. Wrong (B): March 15 is the filing deadline, not the effective date of the revocation. Wrong (D): The revocation would only be deferred to January 1, 2027, if it were filed after March 15 without specifying a prospective date.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q4: medium / L2 — Foreign taxes paid as a separately stated item
(3, 'S Corporations', $EXPL$Hawthorn Corp, an S corporation, reports $160,000 of gross receipts, $90,000 of operating expenses, $12,000 of short-term capital loss, and $9,500 of foreign income taxes paid during 2026. How are the foreign income taxes reported to shareholders?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Deducted in computing ordinary business income on Form 1120-S", "Netted against the short-term capital loss on the corporate return", "Included in the shareholder's stock basis adjustment only", "Separately stated on each shareholder's Schedule K-1"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Under Section 1366(a)(1), foreign taxes paid are separately stated on Schedule K-1 because each shareholder may independently elect to claim a foreign tax credit or deduction on their individual return. The choice affects each shareholder differently, requiring separate reporting. Wrong (A): Foreign taxes are not netted into ordinary business income; they must be passed through separately so shareholders can make the credit-versus-deduction election. Wrong (B): Foreign taxes have no relationship to capital losses; they are separate items with distinct tax treatment. Wrong (C): While foreign taxes do affect basis calculations, they must first be reported as a separately stated item on Schedule K-1.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q5: medium / L3 — Basis ordering with nondeductible life insurance premium
(3, 'S Corporations', $EXPL$Fenwick Corp is an S corporation whose only shareholder is Marcus. His stock basis at January 1 is $60,000. For 2026, Fenwick passes through $15,000 of ordinary income, distributes $40,000 cash, incurs $5,000 of nondeductible officer life insurance premiums on Marcus, and allocates a $35,000 ordinary loss. Applying Section 1367 ordering, what is Marcus's year-end stock basis and suspended loss?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$0 with $5,000 of loss suspended", "$0 with $10,000 of loss suspended", "$5,000 with no suspended loss", "$0 with $35,000 of loss suspended"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under Section 1367, basis is adjusted in this order: (1) increase for income: $60,000 + $15,000 = $75,000; (2) decrease for distributions: $75,000 - $40,000 = $35,000; (3) decrease for nondeductible expenses: $35,000 - $5,000 = $30,000; (4) decrease for deductible losses limited to remaining basis: $30,000 - $35,000, limited to $30,000, leaving $5,000 suspended. Wrong (B): This miscalculates by applying the nondeductible expense in the wrong order or double-counting. Wrong (C): This fails to account for the full $35,000 loss, which exceeds the $30,000 remaining basis after distributions and nondeductible expenses. Wrong (D): This ignores the $15,000 income increase, which must be applied before any decreases.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q6: medium / L3 — AAA computation with nondeductible expense decrease
(3, 'S Corporations', $EXPL$Kensington Corp, an S corporation with beginning AAA of $50,000, has the following 2026 activity: ordinary income of $120,000, tax-exempt municipal bond interest of $8,000, nondeductible regulatory fines of $15,000, and cash distributions totaling $40,000. Compute Kensington's ending AAA balance.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$123,000", "$115,000", "$107,000", "$130,000"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): AAA is increased by ordinary income and decreased by nondeductible expenses and distributions, but is not increased by tax-exempt income. Calculation: $50,000 + $120,000 - $15,000 - $40,000 = $115,000. The $8,000 tax-exempt interest increases shareholder stock basis but does not affect AAA. Wrong (A): This incorrectly adds the $8,000 tax-exempt interest to AAA ($50,000 + $120,000 + $8,000 - $15,000 - $40,000 = $123,000). Wrong (C): This incorrectly subtracts the tax-exempt interest from AAA as well as excluding it from income ($115,000 - $8,000 = $107,000). Wrong (D): This omits the $15,000 nondeductible fine, which must reduce AAA ($50,000 + $120,000 - $40,000 = $130,000).$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q7: easy / L1 — What AAA tracks at the corporate level
(3, 'S Corporations', $EXPL$What does the Accumulated Adjustments Account (AAA) represent for an S corporation?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Each shareholder's individual stock basis in the corporation", "The corporation's retained earnings balance under GAAP", "A corporate-level account tracking cumulative S corporation income that has been taxed to shareholders but not yet distributed", "The total of all shareholder loans made to the corporation"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): The AAA is a corporate-level account that tracks the S corporation's cumulative net income (excluding tax-exempt income) minus distributions. It determines the tax treatment of distributions when the corporation has accumulated C corporation earnings and profits. Wrong (A): Stock basis is a shareholder-level calculation, not a corporate-level account; each shareholder tracks basis individually. Wrong (B): Retained earnings is a GAAP concept unrelated to AAA, which is a tax-specific tracking account. Wrong (D): Shareholder loans create debt basis at the shareholder level; AAA tracks income and distributions, not loan balances.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q8: easy / L1 — Initial stock basis when purchasing from existing shareholder
(3, 'S Corporations', $EXPL$What is a new shareholder's initial stock basis when purchasing S corporation stock from an existing shareholder in an arm's-length transaction?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The S corporation's AAA per share at the date of purchase", "The selling shareholder's adjusted basis in the stock", "The par value of the stock as stated in the corporate charter", "The purchase price paid for the stock"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Under general tax principles, a shareholder who purchases stock from another shareholder in an arm's-length transaction takes a cost basis equal to the purchase price paid. This is independent of the seller's basis, the corporation's AAA, or the stock's par value. Wrong (A): AAA is a corporate-level account used for distribution ordering; it does not determine a purchasing shareholder's stock basis. Wrong (B): The selling shareholder's adjusted basis determines the seller's gain or loss, not the buyer's initial basis; the buyer takes a cost basis. Wrong (C): Par value is a corporate law concept with no bearing on a shareholder's tax basis in the stock.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q9: medium / L3 — Basis with cash contribution, income, distribution, and loss
(3, 'S Corporations', $EXPL$During 2026, Tanner contributes $20,000 cash to Whitmore Corp, an S corporation in which Tanner holds all outstanding stock. Before the contribution, Tanner's stock basis is $15,000. For the year, Whitmore passes through $10,000 of ordinary income, distributes $18,000 cash, and allocates a $30,000 ordinary loss to Tanner. Applying the Section 1367 ordering rules, what is Tanner's year-end stock basis?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$0 with $3,000 of loss suspended", "$0 with $13,000 of loss suspended", "$7,000 with no suspended loss", "$0 with $23,000 of loss suspended"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under Section 1367, apply adjustments in order. Beginning basis: $15,000. Add cash contribution: $15,000 + $20,000 = $35,000. Add income: $35,000 + $10,000 = $45,000. Subtract distribution: $45,000 - $18,000 = $27,000. Subtract loss (limited to remaining basis): $27,000 - $30,000, limited to $27,000, so $3,000 is suspended. Ending basis is $0. Wrong (B): This omits the cash contribution, starting the calculation at $15,000 + $10,000 = $25,000 before distributions and losses. Wrong (C): This applies losses before the distribution, which violates the statutory ordering rules. Wrong (D): This ignores both the contribution and the income increase, applying the full loss against the $15,000 beginning basis.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q10: hard / L4 — Mid-year stock sale combining per-share per-day allocation with basis to compute gain
(3, 'S Corporations', $EXPL$Prescott Corp is an S corporation that reports $365,000 of ordinary income for calendar year 2026 and has 100 shares outstanding. Whitfield holds 25 shares with a beginning basis of $18,000. On May 1, Whitfield sells all 25 shares to Torres for $60,000. The period January 1 through April 30 is 120 days. Under the default per-share, per-day method, what gain does Whitfield recognize?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$42,000", "$12,000", "$30,000", "$60,000"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under Section 1377(a)(1), S corporation income is allocated per-share, per-day. Daily income per share: $365,000 / 365 / 100 = $10. Whitfield's allocated income: 25 shares x 120 days x $10 = $30,000. This increases stock basis from $18,000 to $48,000. Gain on sale: $60,000 - $48,000 = $12,000. Wrong (A): This ignores the per-share, per-day income allocation entirely, computing gain as $60,000 - $18,000 = $42,000. Wrong (C): This confuses the income allocation ($30,000) with the gain; the allocated income increases basis before computing gain. Wrong (D): This treats the entire sale price as gain, ignoring both beginning basis and the income allocation.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4)
;

